> -----Original Message-----
> However, since the wiki is privately hosted what happens if, 
> for instance, the 
> admin decides to close it because he doesn't support any more UML?

The same could be said for either Jeff or yourself.

It wouldn't hurt to have additional mirrors/backups of the data.
However, I view the Wiki administrator as one of the core members of
UML. He has been supporting this project for at least 2 years. In fact,
he has been providing assistance for this project at least as long as
you have.

Rather than question anyone's commitment to the project with potential
"what ifs", we should focus on resolving actual issues.

Initially, we should identify a means for stopping the spam. Once this
is resolved, someone could focus on resolving the global issues faced by
the UML websites. To my knowledge, there are four main sites associated
with "core" information:

http://uml.harlowhill.com/
http://user-mode-linux.sourceforge.net/
http://usermodelinux.org/
http://www.user-mode-linux.org/%7Eblaisorblade/

A better approach would be to unify these under a single domain as a
common repository for information. However, this takes volunteer time
and effort. Perhaps you should consider soliciting the community for a
"web master"? This would allow the work to proceed while leaving you and
Jeff free to pursue the actual technology? This is at least worth a try.

> Many other projects have a wiki as their _main_ website (even because 
> collaborative work on a standard website is more difficult - 
> keeping the 
> sources in CVS is possible but in practice it didn't work for 
> us). But this 
> means that the website admin must be able to act on the wiki.
> 
> Don't misunderstand me, I don't want to refuse help from the 
> community or 
> consider it less, nor to criticize anybody, but the wiki _is_ 
> important, and 
> it should have a more central role.

I agree that it should have a more central role. However, I disagree
with the assumption that anyone besides you and Jeff are of questionable
persistence. Many people have coughed up resources, time and support for
the project over the past several years. While a more organized approach
is in order, it doesn't mean that you can't trust anyone besides Jeff.

Tony

